        "question": "Describe how mass participation events provide opportunities for business.",
        "marks": 8,
        "model": "Mass participation events create a large, identifiable market on a fixed date, and businesses can plan around it. Local hospitality and retail — hotels, restaurants, cafés and shops — take substantial extra income from the thousands of participants and supporters who travel to and stay in the host town, and many take on additional staff for the period. Sports retailers and service providers sell into a defined need in the weeks beforehand: footwear, clothing, nutrition, training plans, physiotherapy and coaching. The event itself is a business, generating income from entry fees, merchandise, timing services, photography and catering concessions, and supporting an events industry that employs organisers, security and medical providers. It is also a valuable sponsorship platform, because a brand that titles the event reaches a precisely chosen audience of health-conscious adults through every entry form, race number and item of coverage, at a far lower cost per contact than general advertising. Because most of these events recur annually, businesses can invest in capacity and build the relationship year on year rather than treating it as a one-off.",
