        "label": "(a) (ii)",
        "question": "Describe the appropriate scoring or recording system of a named physical activity of your choice.",
        "marks": 5,
        "model": "Named physical activity: basketball. Scoring is by points, and the value depends on where the shot is taken from and the situation. A field goal made from inside the three-point arc is worth two points; a field goal made with both feet behind the arc is worth three; and a free throw, awarded after a foul, is worth one point each. The scorer's table records every score as it is made, along with fouls, timeouts and substitutions, and the running total is shown on the scoreboard with the game clock and the shot clock. Officials on court signal each score to the table — three fingers raised for a successful three-pointer — so the record is confirmed rather than assumed. The team with the higher total at the end of the fourth quarter wins; if the scores are level, overtime periods of five minutes are played until one team leads at the end of a period. The official record is the scoresheet signed by the officials at the end of the game, not the scoreboard.",
