        "model": "Cardiovascular endurance is the single most important factor because the 1500m is run at close to VO2 max for around four minutes. The rate at which oxygen can be delivered to and used by the working muscles determines the pace that can be sustained before anaerobic metabolism and its by-products force a slowdown. No amount of tactical awareness compensates for an athlete who cannot hold the pace of the field, so aerobic capacity sets the ceiling on performance.\nPacing and tactical decision-making is the second most important because championship 1500m races are rarely won by the athlete with the best personal best. The race is decided by decisions made under fatigue: where to position in the pack to avoid being boxed in, whether to cover a mid-race move, and when to begin the final kick. An athlete who runs the first lap too fast destroys their own finish, and one who is badly positioned entering the last bend cannot use the fitness they have. Fitness creates the possibility; the tactical decisions determine whether it is converted into a result.",
