        "question": "Positive outcomes were achieved from a modest school-community linked intervention.\nOutline two ways in which pathways between school and community based physical activity could be strengthened.",
        "marks": 8,
        "model": "1. Formal club–school links with a shared coach. A local club provides a qualified, vetted coach to deliver a block of PE lessons or an after-school session in the school, finishing with an open invitation to a club session and a named contact. This works because the biggest barrier to a young person joining a club is not interest but the unfamiliarity of turning up alone; meeting the coach in a familiar setting first removes it, and the school gains coaching expertise it may not have.\n2. Shared facilities and coordinated timetabling. The school opens its hall and pitches to community clubs outside school hours, and the local authority or Local Sports Partnership timetables community sessions to run immediately after school on the school site. This addresses cost, transport and supervision in one move — the pupil does not have to travel or be collected — and it makes the school a year-round community facility rather than one that is locked at four o'clock.",
