        "question": "Nikki Hamblin (New Zealand) and Abbey D’Agostino (United States) are runners who received an award from the International Fair Play Committee for helping each other finish after they both fell during a 5000m race at the 2016 Rio Olympic Games.\n‘Sportsmanship may lead to enhanced engagement in physical activity, while gamesmanship may contribute to lowering levels of engagement in physical activity’.",
        "marks": 8,
        "model": "The statement holds. Sportsmanship enhances engagement because it makes participation a positive experience that people want to repeat. When opponents help each other, respect officials and accept results, the environment is safe and welcoming, which matters most for beginners and young players deciding whether sport is for them. Acts like Hamblin and D'Agostino's are widely reported and reach far beyond the event, presenting sport as something worth belonging to; parents are more willing to place children in clubs with that culture, and volunteers, coaches and officials are more willing to stay. Retention of officials is significant in itself, since a shortage of referees directly reduces the number of games that can be played.\nGamesmanship lowers engagement because it changes what participation feels like. Diving, sledging, time-wasting and intimidation make the experience unpleasant and unfair, and players who do not want to compete that way drop out. It corrodes trust in results, so success feels arbitrary. It falls hardest on officials, who absorb the dissent and abuse that gamesmanship generates, and abuse of referees is the most cited reason for officials leaving the game. It also transmits downwards: young players copy what they see elite performers do successfully, so gamesmanship at the top reappears in underage sport, where its effect on drop-out is greatest.",
