        "model": "Newton's laws are used in movement analysis because they explain why a movement produces the result it does, rather than simply describing what it looked like. They identify the forces acting, so a coach can locate the cause of a fault: if a sprinter is slow out of the blocks, the second law shows the problem is either insufficient force applied or force applied in the wrong direction, and the third law shows that force must be directed backwards and downwards into the blocks to produce forward motion. They allow performance to be quantified and compared, since force, mass and acceleration can be measured, so changes can be judged objectively rather than by opinion. They also guide technique and equipment decisions — reducing the mass that must be accelerated, extending the time over which force is applied to reduce impact, or altering the angle of application. Finally, understanding the forces involved informs safe practice, particularly in landing and contact, where the size and duration of the reaction force determine injury risk.",
