        "label": "",
        "question": "Israel Olatunde became Ireland’s fastest man over 100m by running a time of 10.17 seconds at the European Athletics Championships in Munich on 16 August 2022.\nDescribe two principles of effective practice that could have been utilised in the design of Olatunde’s practice schedules.",
        "marks": 8,
        "model": "1. Specificity of practice. Practice must reproduce the demands of the competitive event. For a 100m sprinter this means maximal-velocity work from blocks over race distances with full recovery, rather than general endurance running: the ATP-PC system, the movement pattern and the intensity in practice must match the ten seconds of the race, because adaptation is specific to the demand imposed.\n2. Appropriate practice distribution and progression. Because sprinting is a maximal, neurally demanding skill, effective practice is distributed rather than massed — short blocks of high-quality efforts separated by long recovery, so every repetition is performed at competition speed and quality does not degrade with fatigue. Practice also progresses from part to whole and from simple to complex: block clearance and drive phase are refined separately before being integrated into full race runs, with feedback and video used to correct one cue at a time.",
