        "question": "Describe how any two energy systems used by the human body may contribute to performance in a physical activity of your choice.",
        "marks": 8,
        "model": "Physical activity: soccer.\nATP-PC (anaerobic alactic) system: stored ATP and phosphocreatine in the muscle are broken down without oxygen to resynthesise ATP extremely rapidly, supplying maximal efforts lasting up to about ten seconds. In soccer this powers the sprint onto a through ball, the jump for a header and the shot. Its capacity is very small, so it depletes within seconds and must be restored during low-intensity periods of play.\nAerobic (oxidative) system: carbohydrate and fat are broken down in the presence of oxygen in the mitochondria, producing large amounts of ATP slowly and without fatiguing by-products. In soccer it supplies the walking and jogging that make up most of the 10–12 km a midfielder covers in a match, and — just as importantly — it is the system that resynthesises phosphocreatine and clears lactate between high-intensity efforts. A player with a strong aerobic system recovers faster between sprints and can therefore repeat them more often late in the game.",
