        "model": "Physical activity: golf. Selection of equipment matters because equipment must match the individual performer and the demands of the activity. Clubs are fitted to the player's height, swing speed and swing path — shaft length, flex, lie angle and grip size are all adjusted — because a shaft too stiff for a player's swing speed loses distance, and an incorrect lie angle sends the ball offline regardless of how well the shot is struck. The set is also chosen for the course and conditions: the number of wedges and their loft, and the choice of ball, are selected for the shots the player actually faces. Correct selection therefore determines whether the player's technique produces the intended result, and poorly selected equipment builds compensations into the swing as the player unconsciously adjusts to it. Safety and rules also govern selection: clubs and balls must conform to the R&A specification or scores do not stand. Maintenance matters just as much, because equipment degrades in ways that are gradual and easy to miss. Grips harden and lose tackiness over a season, so the player grips more tightly, tension rises through the arms and the swing changes. Grooves on wedges wear smooth, reducing spin and control on approach shots. Shafts can be damaged, and a loose head is a safety risk to the player and others. Regular cleaning of grooves, annual regripping, checking shafts and heads, and keeping the bag dry are all part of maintaining performance. In an activity where the margins are small, well-chosen and well-maintained equipment is a precondition for the technique to work, and it also protects the substantial financial investment the equipment represents.",
