        "question": "Examine two nutritional considerations that an athlete might implement after performance to enhance recovery. You are not permitted to use hydration or sports drinks.",
        "marks": 10,
        "model": "1. Carbohydrate replacement to restore muscle glycogen. Intense or prolonged performance substantially depletes the glycogen stored in the working muscles, and glycogen is the fuel that limits the next session. The athlete should take in carbohydrate soon after finishing — roughly 1 to 1.2 grams per kilogram of body mass in the first hour, repeated over the following hours — because the muscle is most receptive to glucose uptake immediately after exercise, when glycogen synthase activity and insulin sensitivity are elevated. Practically this means a carbohydrate-rich snack such as porridge, a bagel, rice or fruit within the first half hour, followed by a full meal. The timing matters most when the athlete is training or competing again within 24 hours; where recovery time is longer, total daily carbohydrate matters more than the timing of the first feed.\n2. Protein intake to support repair and adaptation. Hard training damages muscle fibres and raises the rate of protein breakdown; the adaptation the session was intended to produce depends on protein synthesis exceeding that breakdown afterwards. The athlete should take in roughly 0.3 grams per kilogram of high-quality protein — around 20 to 25 grams for most athletes — soon after finishing, from a source rich in leucine such as milk, yoghurt, eggs, chicken or fish, and repeat this every three to four hours across the day rather than taking it all in one meal. Combining the protein with the recovery carbohydrate is efficient in practice and does not impair glycogen restoration. Adequate total daily protein spread across meals, including a serving before sleep, is more effective than any single post-exercise dose.",
