        "question": "Describe two strategies that could be used to aid recovery after a training session.",
        "marks": 8,
        "model": "1. Active cool-down followed by refuelling. Five to ten minutes of light jogging or cycling, then gentle stretching, keeps blood flowing through the muscles, prevents blood pooling and helps clear metabolic by-products. Within the following half hour the athlete takes in carbohydrate to replace the glycogen used and protein to repair damaged muscle fibres — for example a bowl of porridge and a glass of milk, or a chicken sandwich — along with fluid and electrolytes to replace what was lost in sweat.\n2. Sleep and planned rest. Adaptation to training happens during recovery rather than during the session, and most of the body's repair processes, including growth hormone release, occur during deep sleep. Getting eight or more hours after a hard session, and following a heavy day with an easier one or a rest day, allows the muscle damage to be repaired and the training to be absorbed. Without it the athlete simply accumulates fatigue and performance declines.",
