        "label": "(b)",
        "question": "Name and explain three different principles of training.",
        "marks": 12,
        "model": "1. Specificity. Training must match the demands of the activity — the same muscle groups, movement patterns, energy systems and intensities used in competition. Adaptation is specific to the stimulus applied, so a sprinter who trains only with long slow running will develop aerobic fitness that does not transfer to a ten-second race. Applying specificity means the training looks like the activity it is preparing for.\n2. Progressive overload. The training load must be increased gradually over time, by raising intensity, duration or frequency, because the body only adapts when the demand exceeds what it is already accustomed to. Without it an athlete plateaus; increased too quickly, it causes injury and overtraining rather than improvement, so the increase is made in planned steps with recovery built in.\n3. Reversibility. Fitness gains are lost if training stops or the load falls too far — the body de-adapts, and cardiovascular fitness in particular declines within a few weeks of stopping. This is why a plan must include maintenance work during the off-season and during injury rehabilitation, and why a long break costs more than the time it takes to recover it.",
