        "question": "Why is periodisation used in sports coaching?",
        "marks": 4,
        "model": "Periodisation is used because an athlete cannot train hard at everything all year and cannot hold peak condition for more than a short period. Dividing the year into phases allows the coach to develop one quality at a time in a sensible order — building a base of general fitness and strength first, then converting it into the speed and power the sport needs, then sharpening in competition. It allows the athlete to peak at the right time, because the plan works backwards from the target event and reduces load in a planned taper beforehand so the athlete arrives fit and fresh rather than fatigued. It builds recovery in deliberately, alternating harder and easier weeks, which reduces the risk of overtraining, illness and injury. Varying the training also prevents staleness and keeps the athlete mentally engaged, and because each phase has a stated aim, progress can be monitored and the plan adjusted.",
