      {
        "label": "(a) (ii)",
        "question": "Give a reason for selecting each nutrient.\nNutrients before the event Reason(s) Nutrient during the event Reason(s) Nutrients after the event Reason(s)",
        "marks": 10,
        "model": "Before the event — carbohydrate: it is the body's preferred fuel at high intensity, and a meal three hours beforehand tops up the muscle and liver glycogen the player will rely on for repeated sprinting. Low-fat and low-fibre choices are used so digestion is complete before throw-in. Fluid and electrolytes: starting the game properly hydrated maintains blood volume and the ability to sweat and lose heat, since even mild dehydration reduces sprint performance and decision-making.\nDuring the event — fast-acting carbohydrate: a sports drink or gel at half time maintains blood glucose late in the game, which delays fatigue and preserves skill execution and concentration in the final quarter. Fluid and electrolytes: fluid replaces sweat losses to prevent a fall in blood volume and a rise in core temperature; sodium in the drink helps retain the fluid and replaces what is lost in sweat.\nAfter the event — carbohydrate: it refills the muscle glycogen used during the match, and the muscle takes up glucose most readily in the first hour afterwards, which matters when the next match or session is within a few days. Protein: it repairs the muscle damage caused by sprinting, striking and contact, and supports the adaptation to the workload. Fluid and electrolytes: they restore the body's fluid balance, and roughly one and a half times the fluid lost is needed because some is passed as urine.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 22,
        "model_source": "scheme"
      },
      {
        "label": "(b)",
        "question": "For each of the nutrients named in (a), give an example of a type of food that could be eaten on the day of the event to ensure that the athlete gets the appropriate nutrient.\nBefore the event nutrient Food During the event nutrient Food After the event nutrient Food",
        "marks": 10,
        "model": "Before the event — carbohydrate: a bowl of porridge, or pasta with a light tomato sauce and chicken.\nDuring the event — fast-acting carbohydrate: an isotonic sports drink, or a banana at half time.\nAfter the event — carbohydrate and protein together: a chicken and rice meal, or a chocolate milk followed by a chicken sandwich.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 22,
        "model_source": "scheme"
      },
      {
        "label": "(c)",
        "question": "Explain two psychological factors from the list below and describe the impact that they can have on performance.\nConfidence Anxiety Motivation Concentration Explanation Impact on performance Explanation Impact on performance",
        "marks": 10,
        "model": "Factor 1: Confidence. Confidence is a performer's belief in their own ability to carry out the skills required and achieve the outcome they are aiming for.\nImpact on performance: a confident performer attempts difficult skills and commits fully to them, which makes them more likely to succeed; they persist after an error rather than becoming tentative, and they make decisions earlier because they are not worrying about consequences. Confidence also keeps arousal in the optimal range, reducing the muscular tension that disrupts fine motor control. Low confidence produces hesitation, safe and negative choices, and a self-fulfilling cycle in which poor performance further reduces belief.\nFactor 2: Anxiety. Anxiety is a state of worry, nervousness or apprehension in response to a perceived threat, presenting as worrying thoughts, physical symptoms such as raised heart rate and muscle tension, and behaviours such as fidgeting or rushing.\nImpact on performance: a moderate level raises arousal and alertness and can sharpen reaction time and effort. Excessive anxiety, however, raises muscle tension and disrupts the fine control needed for skills such as a free or a penalty, narrows and misdirects attention onto the crowd or the consequences of failure so relevant cues are missed, and slows decision-making. At its extreme it produces choking, where a well-learned skill breaks down because the performer starts consciously controlling it again.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 22,
        "model_source": "scheme"
      }
