      {
        "label": "(a)",
        "question": "The principles of effective practice are important in the design of skill practice sessions.\nOutline two principles of effective practice.",
        "marks": 4,
        "model": "1. Specificity of practice — the practice must reproduce the demands of the competitive situation, using the same skills, speeds, energy systems and conditions in which the skill will actually be performed, so that the learning transfers into performance.\n2. Appropriate progression — practice moves from simple to complex and from part to whole, and from closed, unopposed conditions to open, opposed and game-speed conditions, so that the demand rises only when the previous stage is secure. (Variability of practice, appropriate distribution of practice and rest, sufficient meaningful repetitions, and use of feedback are equally acceptable.)",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 7,
        "model_source": "scheme"
      },
      {
        "label": "(b)",
        "question": "How would you use one of the principles outlined by you in Question 6 (a) to develop skill in a practice session?\nPrinciple of effective practice:",
        "marks": 4,
        "model": "Principle of effective practice: appropriate progression. Physical activity: basketball, developing the lay-up. I would begin with the skill in its simplest closed form — the player takes the ball from a standing start beside the basket with no dribble and no defender, and performs the footwork and release only. Once the footwork is consistent, I would add one dribble into the same movement, then the approach from the wing at jogging pace, then at game speed. Only when that is secure would I add a passive defender who applies token pressure, then an active defender contesting the shot, and finally the lay-up taken at the end of a fast break with fatigue present. Each stage is held until the player is successful most of the time, and I would move back a stage if execution breaks down.\nThis works because the player is never asked to attend to more than they can manage: at the start all attention is on the movement pattern, and the defender is introduced only once the movement no longer requires conscious control. Progression also keeps the player successful, which sustains motivation, and it finishes in conditions that resemble the game, so the skill transfers rather than only working in isolation.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 7,
        "model_source": "scheme"
      }
