        "label": "(a) (ii)",
        "question": "Discuss the personal and social benefits, mentioned in the case study, for children engaging in throwing activities.",
        "marks": 5,
        "model": "Personal benefits: throwing activities develop whole-body strength, power and coordination, and because the implements are thrown with the whole kinetic chain they build core stability and force transfer that transfer to many other activities. Children develop fundamental movement skills — overarm throwing in particular is a basic movement pattern that underpins a wide range of sports and which many children never properly acquire. There is a strong motivational benefit too: throwing produces an immediate, measurable result, so a child can see improvement session by session, which builds competence and self-confidence. Throwing events also suit a range of body types, so children who are unsuccessful in running-based activities can find something they are good at, which keeps them in sport rather than losing them.\nSocial benefits: throwing is taught and practised in small groups within a club or school athletics setting, so children work alongside others, take turns, encourage each other and learn to compete without direct physical contest. Athletics clubs give a sense of belonging and bring children into contact with coaches and volunteers as role models beyond the family. Because performance is measured individually against a distance rather than decided by selection for a team, every child gets to take part rather than sitting on a bench, which is particularly important for retaining less able children. Competition days provide a shared occasion for families and clubs, strengthening the community around the sport.",
