        "question": "Name a skill that uses a 3rd class lever from a named physical activity of your choice.\nJustify why this skill is an example of a 3rd class lever.",
        "marks": 5,
        "model": "Named physical activity: basketball. Skill: the chest pass (elbow extension phase); or in flexion, the biceps curl used in preparing the pass.\nJustification: taking elbow flexion when bringing the ball towards the body, the fulcrum is the elbow joint, the effort is applied by the biceps brachii at its insertion on the radius just below the joint, and the load is the combined weight of the forearm, hand and ball at the far end of the limb. Because the effort is applied between the fulcrum and the load, this is by definition a third class lever. The consequence is that the effort arm is much shorter than the load arm, so the mechanical advantage is less than one and the muscle must produce a force considerably greater than the load it moves. What the arrangement gives in return is speed and range: a small, fast shortening of the biceps produces a large and rapid movement of the hand, which is exactly what a quick pass requires. Almost all limb actions in sport are third class levers for this reason.",
