        "question": "Explain how you would apply the practice method named by you in Question 13 (c) (i) to improve the skill of a javelin thrower.",
        "marks": 5,
        "model": "Applying fixed (massed) whole practice to the javelin: the thrower repeats the complete throw from the same run-up, on the same runway, with the same implement, under the same conditions, for a set number of quality repetitions in each session. The conditions never vary, which is appropriate because the competitive skill never varies either — the javelin throw is a closed skill performed in a stable environment, so there is no need to build variability into it.\nIn practice I would organise it as follows. The thrower performs blocks of throws with a full recovery between each, because the throw is a maximal effort and quality falls quickly with fatigue; a small number of high-quality throws is worth more than a large number of tired ones. The coach fixes attention on one technical cue at a time — for example the position of the blocking leg at delivery — and gives specific extrinsic feedback immediately after each throw, so the correction can be applied on the very next repetition. Video is used so the thrower can see the fault rather than only hear about it. Standing throws and short-approach throws are used at the start of the session to groove the delivery position before the full run-up is added, and light and heavy implements are introduced to develop the specific speed and strength the throw requires. The session finishes with full-approach throws at competition intensity so the practice ends in the conditions in which the skill is performed.",
