        "label": "(d) (i)",
        "question": "Interestingly, many throwing coaches now advocate for the throwing of both light and heavy implements in training. It is believed that these training approaches can help to develop different components of fitness relevant to the javelin.\nUse Figure 9 to suggest which component of fitness might be developed by throwing light implements in training.\nJustify why this component of fitness might be developed by throwing light implements.",
        "marks": 5,
        "model": "Throwing a lighter implement in training develops speed, and specifically the speed of movement and rate of force development. Because the implement is lighter than the competition weight, the thrower can move it faster than they can move the standard javelin, so the whole throwing action is performed at a higher velocity than competition allows. This overspeed work trains the nervous system to fire the sequence more rapidly and to coordinate the kinetic chain at a speed the athlete cannot otherwise reach. Since release velocity is the single largest determinant of the distance a javelin travels, training the speed side of the power equation is directly relevant to performance.",
