        "marks": 8,
        "model": "Physical activity: ice hockey. The ATP-PC (anaerobic alactic) system supplies energy for the first 8–10 seconds of a shift: stored phosphocreatine resynthesises ATP very rapidly without oxygen, powering the explosive first strides, a body-check and a slap shot. The anaerobic glycolytic system then takes over for the remainder of a 40–60 second shift, breaking down glucose without oxygen to sustain high-intensity skating, but producing lactate and hydrogen ions that cause fatigue. The aerobic system dominates on the bench and between periods, using oxygen to resynthesise ATP and to clear metabolic by-products so the player can repeat the next shift.",
