        "model": "Physical activity: hurling. Physical demands: the game is intermittent and repeated-sprint in nature, so a player needs a high aerobic base to cover 8–10 km and to recover between efforts, the ATP-PC and glycolytic systems for repeated 5–30 metre sprints, muscular power for striking and jumping, and agility to change direction in contact. Technical demands: striking off both sides from the hand and the ground, controlling a dropping ball on the hurley, hooking and blocking, first-touch under pressure, and accurate long and short passing. The two interact — technique degrades as fatigue rises, so conditioning is what allows the skills to hold up in the final quarter, which is where matches are decided.",
