      {
        "label": "(b)",
        "question": "the 2019 Irish Sports Monitor Report, illustrates the % of Irish males and females that participated in a sport in 2015, 2017 and 2019.\nExamine how women or another group (from the list below) might tackle the barriers that exist for them in relation to participation in physical activity:\n• Older Adults\n• Different ethnic groups\n• People with physical disability\n• Different socio‐economic groups\n• People with intellectual disability.\nGroup",
        "marks": 5,
        "model": "Group: Women. The barriers evident in participation data are time and caring responsibilities, cost, body image and confidence, safety, and low visibility of female role models and competition. These can be tackled from both sides. Provision can change: women-only sessions and beginner-level entry points reduce the confidence barrier, sessions timed around school and work with crèche facilities address the time barrier, and lit, overlooked walking and running routes address safety. Women themselves can tackle barriers by using social supports — a running group or club buddy system creates commitment and makes participation sociable rather than an individual effort — and by choosing low-cost, low-equipment activities such as walking, parkrun or community fitness classes. The growth of visible female competition and campaigns such as 20x20 has widened what girls see as available to them, which shifts the cultural barrier over time.",
        "diagram": "exam-images/pe/2022-HL-fig13.png",
        "source_page": 26,
        "model_source": "scheme"
      },
      {
        "label": "(c) (i)",
        "question": "Anxiety is a concept that is widely discussed by performers and coaches. Practitioners involved in sports performance need to be aware of anxiety related symptoms. Once awareness is built it would be prudent to deal with anxiety related issues.\nDefine the psychological factor anxiety.",
        "marks": 5,
        "model": "Anxiety is a state of nervousness, worry or apprehension in response to a perceived threat or demand. It can present in cognitive form (worrying thoughts, doubt, loss of concentration), somatic form (raised heart rate, sweating, muscle tension, nausea) and behavioural form (fidgeting, rushing, avoidance).",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 26,
        "model_source": "scheme"
      },
      {
        "label": "(c) (ii)",
        "question": "Discuss how anxiety might impact an athlete’s performance.",
        "marks": 5,
        "model": "Anxiety can impair or, at moderate levels, sharpen performance. Excessive somatic anxiety raises muscle tension and heart rate, which disrupts the fine motor control needed for a free throw or a penalty and causes technique to become rushed and rigid. Cognitive anxiety narrows and misdirects attention: the performer attends to the consequences of failure or to the crowd rather than the relevant cue, so decision-making slows and errors increase. It also raises the chance of choking, where a well-learned skill is consciously controlled again and breaks down. A moderate level of anxiety, however, raises arousal into the optimal band, increasing alertness, reaction speed and effort — so the coach's aim is regulation rather than elimination.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 26,
        "model_source": "scheme"
      },
      {
        "label": "(c) (iii)",
        "question": "Describe how another psychological factor that you have studied might impact an athlete’s performance.",
        "marks": 5,
        "model": "Psychological factor: motivation. Intrinsic motivation — taking part for enjoyment, mastery and personal satisfaction — sustains the volume of quality practice that improvement requires, and it keeps a performer training through injury rehabilitation or a poor run of results when no external reward is available. Extrinsic motivation from selection, medals, prize money or a sponsor's expectations can raise short-term effort and focus, but if it becomes the only driver it is associated with higher anxiety, greater risk of burnout and drop-out once the reward is removed. Coaches therefore use goal setting — specific, measurable, process-focused targets — to build competence and keep motivation internal.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 26,
        "model_source": "scheme"
      },
      {
        "label": "(d) (i)",
        "question": "You are writing a code of practice document for a youth sports club.\nState three criteria that you would include in the document for the selection of a commercial sponsor for the club.",
        "marks": 5,
        "model": "(1) The sponsor's products and business must be appropriate for a youth audience — no alcohol, gambling, tobacco, vaping or high-fat/high-sugar food brands. (2) The sponsor's stated values and conduct must align with the club's code of ethics, including child safeguarding, inclusion and fair play. (3) The agreement must not compromise the club's independence: the club retains control of selection, coaching decisions, playing time and its own identity, and the commitments required of young players must be limited and age-appropriate.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 26,
        "model_source": "scheme"
      },
      {
        "label": "(d) (ii)",
        "question": "Choose two of the criteria stated by you in (i) and outline why you would include these in your code of practice for selecting a commercial sponsor for a youth sports club.\n“I don’t care how concerned McDonald’s are about grass‐ roots sports, the only thing my kids’ think when they see the Golden Arches is what burger and fries combo they want me to buy them, not kicking a footy around!”",
        "marks": 10,
        "model": "Criterion 1 — product appropriateness. A youth club's members are children, and a sponsor's logo on a jersey is a persistent endorsement to them and to everyone watching. Accepting a fast-food or gambling sponsor would directly contradict the health and wellbeing message the club exists to promote, and would expose children to marketing they cannot critically evaluate — exactly the concern the parent quoted raises about the Golden Arches. Criterion 2 — protecting the club's independence. Sponsorship income creates leverage. Without a written limit, a sponsor can begin to influence team names, playing kit, fixture scheduling or even selection to suit commercial ends. Stating in advance that the club retains full control of all sporting and safeguarding decisions makes that boundary explicit before money changes hands, and protects both the young players and the volunteers running the club.",
        "diagram": "exam-images/pe/2022-HL-fig14.png",
        "source_page": 26,
        "model_source": "scheme"
      }
