        "label": "(a) (ii)",
        "question": "Why is flexibility important for a tennis player?",
        "marks": 4,
        "model": "Flexibility is important for a tennis player because the game constantly demands movement at the limit of the player's range. A greater range of motion at the shoulder allows a fuller backswing and a higher, more extended contact point on the serve, which produces more racket-head speed and a better angle over the net. Flexibility through the hips, trunk and hamstrings lets the player lunge wide and low to reach a drop shot or a wide ball and still return it under control, so more balls are reached and fewer points conceded. It also allows full trunk rotation into a forehand or a two-handed backhand, which is where much of the power comes from. Just as importantly, flexibility reduces injury risk: the sudden stretches, lunges and rapid changes of direction in tennis place muscles and tendons at the end of their range, and a player without adequate flexibility is far more likely to strain a hamstring, groin or shoulder.",
