        "model": "Nutrient: carbohydrate. An athlete eats carbohydrate because it is the body's main and preferred fuel for exercise, particularly at moderate and high intensity. It is stored in the muscles and liver as glycogen, and the size of that store determines how long the athlete can maintain their intensity before fatiguing. Carbohydrate is also the only fuel the brain uses in normal conditions, so as blood glucose falls, concentration, decision-making and skill execution deteriorate as well as physical output. An athlete therefore eats carbohydrate before an event to fill the glycogen store, during prolonged events to maintain blood glucose, and after an event to refill what has been used ready for the next session.",
