        "label": "(a) (ii)",
        "question": "Give a possible reason why 64% of students play Gaelic games but only 2% go swimming.\nResult 1 Result 2 Result 3",
        "marks": 4,
        "model": "The difference is largely one of provision and access rather than of preference. Gaelic games are available in almost every rural parish through a local GAA club, at low cost, with pitches within walking distance, volunteer coaches, and school teams that feed directly into the club — so a student can take part without transport, expense or specialist facilities. Swimming requires a pool, and in a rural area the nearest pool may be a long drive away, with entry fees, lesson costs and transport that a family must fund and organise. There is also a cultural factor: Gaelic games are embedded in the community, so children grow up with the expectation of playing and with family and friends already involved, whereas swimming has no comparable local tradition. Confidence matters too — swimming requires a learned competence that a student who never had lessons will not have, and self-consciousness about swimwear deters some teenagers from taking part at all.",
