        "label": "(a) (iii)",
        "question": "Discuss why there is a change in the participation levels of year groups (Result 3 in",
        "marks": 8,
        "model": "Participation falls sharply between first year and sixth year, from 87% to 21%. Several factors combine to produce this. Academic pressure is the most cited: as students move towards the Leaving Certificate, study displaces training and matches, and both families and students often treat sport as the thing to drop. Competition and selection play a part — as teams become more competitive, students who are not selected stop attending, so the students most in need of activity are the first to leave. Interests change: older teenagers take up part-time work, social activities and screen-based leisure, and school sport competes with all of them. Provision narrows too, since schools generally offer a wide range of activity to junior years and a smaller, more competitive programme to senior years, and PE is sometimes reduced or dropped from the senior timetable. Confidence and body image become more significant in the later teenage years, particularly for girls. Finally, students who have not found an activity they enjoy by senior cycle have little reason to continue, which is why breadth of provision in junior cycle matters so much.",
