        "question": "Explain Newton’s Third Law of Motion, using a named physical activity of your choice.",
        "marks": 6,
        "model": "Newton's Third Law of Motion states that for every action there is an equal and opposite reaction: when one body exerts a force on a second body, the second body exerts a force of equal size and opposite direction back on the first.\nNamed physical activity: swimming. In the front crawl the swimmer's hand and forearm push water backwards during the pull. The water exerts an equal and opposite force forwards on the swimmer, and it is this reaction force that propels them along the pool. The same law governs the start: the swimmer pushes downwards and backwards into the blocks, and the blocks push the swimmer upwards and forwards with an equal and opposite force. This explains why technique matters so much — the larger the surface area of the hand and forearm and the more directly backwards the water is pushed, the greater the forward reaction force. A hand that slips sideways through the water applies force in the wrong direction and produces little propulsion.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 27,
        "model_source": "scheme"
