        "label": "(c) (ii)",
        "question": "Name and explain another one of Newton’s Laws of Motion.",
        "marks": 4,
        "model": "Newton's First Law of Motion (the law of inertia): a body will remain at rest, or continue moving at constant velocity in a straight line, unless acted upon by an external force.\nIn swimming, a swimmer resting on the blocks stays at rest until the muscular force of the leg drive acts on them. Once moving, the swimmer would in theory continue at the same velocity indefinitely, but the water applies a constant external force — drag — which slows them, and this is why a swimmer must keep applying propulsive force and why streamlining off the wall matters so much: reducing drag reduces the external force acting to slow them. The same law explains why a ball placed on the ground does not move until it is kicked, and why it eventually stops when friction and air resistance act on it.",
