        "marks": 8,
        "model": "1. Named physical activity: swimming — full-body polyurethane and high-technology swimsuits, and the pool and starting-block design that accompanied them. The suits compressed the body, trapped air and reduced drag in the water, and in 2008 and 2009 they contributed to an extraordinary number of world records being set. Deeper pools, wave-reducing lane ropes and angled starting blocks with a rear footplate also reduced turbulence and improved the start. The performance effect was large enough that FINA banned full-body non-textile suits from 2010, which is itself evidence of the size of the change; the block and pool improvements remain and continue to benefit performance.\n2. Named physical activity: distance running — carbon-plated racing shoes with high-stack, high-energy-return foam. These shoes reduce the energy cost of running by returning a proportion of the energy absorbed at each footstrike and by stiffening the foot's lever, and independent research has measured improvements in running economy of several per cent. Their introduction coincided with a wave of road and marathon records at both elite and amateur level, to the extent that World Athletics introduced limits on stack height and plate number. The technology has changed both performance and the equipment rules that govern it, and it has raised a question about fairness, since access to expensive shoes now affects results.",
