        "label": "(c) (i)",
        "question": "Marta ran a 10km race wearing new runners. She ended up with blisters and a knee injury. Explain the importance of selecting suitable attire in a named physical activity of your choice. You cannot use running.",
        "marks": 6,
        "model": "Named physical activity: hurling. Selecting suitable attire matters for both performance and safety. Footwear must match the surface and the demands of the game: moulded or studded boots that grip a wet grass pitch allow a player to accelerate, turn and change direction without slipping, whereas unsuitable footwear causes both a loss of performance and a high risk of ankle and knee injury from slipping or from studs catching. Boots must also fit properly and be broken in — Marta's blisters and knee injury are exactly what happens when new footwear is worn for a full effort without being worn in, because friction causes blisters and a change in cushioning or support alters running mechanics and loads the knee differently.\nProtective attire is compulsory rather than optional: a helmet with a faceguard meeting the required standard must be worn correctly with the chin strap fastened, and a gum shield protects the teeth and jaw. Clothing must suit the conditions — layers and warm gear in cold or wet weather to maintain muscle temperature and prevent stiffness, breathable material in heat to allow sweat to evaporate — and it must allow full range of movement, since kit that is too tight restricts the striking action. Jewellery must be removed, and the jersey colour must contrast with the opposition's so play is not confused. Attire that is unsuitable therefore costs performance, causes avoidable injury, and in the case of protective equipment breaches the rules of the game.",
