        "label": "(c) (ii)",
        "question": "Give an example of how you would maintain a sporting facility and why this is important.",
        "marks": 4,
        "model": "Example: regular inspection and maintenance of a grass pitch — mowing to the correct height, repairing divots and worn areas at the goalmouths, aerating and sanding to maintain drainage, re-marking the lines, and checking that goalposts are securely anchored, padded and undamaged before every use.\nThis is important first for safety: an uneven surface with holes or worn patches is the most common cause of ankle and knee injuries, poor drainage leaves surface water that causes slipping, and an unsecured or damaged goalpost is a serious hazard capable of causing a fatality. Second, it protects performance and the quality of the game, since a true, level surface allows the ball to run predictably and lets players use their technique. Third, it protects the club's investment: a pitch that is maintained continuously lasts far longer and costs far less than one repaired only after it has deteriorated, and it can be used for more fixtures per season. Fourth, it is a legal and insurance obligation — a club has a duty of care to those using its facility, and insurance cover generally depends on being able to show that regular inspection and maintenance were carried out.",
