        "marks": 4,
        "model": "A Therapeutic Use Exemption is formal permission granted to an athlete to use a substance or method that appears on the WADA Prohibited List, because it is needed to treat a diagnosed medical condition. The athlete applies in advance to the relevant anti-doping organisation and an independent panel of doctors decides. It is granted only where four conditions are met: the athlete would experience a significant impairment to health without the medication; the treatment produces no performance enhancement beyond returning the athlete to normal health; there is no reasonable permitted alternative; and the need is not the consequence of previously using a prohibited substance.\nThey are used where an athlete has a genuine medical condition whose standard treatment is prohibited — for example insulin for type 1 diabetes, an inhaled or systemic corticosteroid or a beta-2 agonist above the permitted threshold for severe asthma, or a stimulant prescribed for ADHD. Where a TUE has been granted, the presence of that substance in a sample is not an anti-doping rule violation.",
