        "question": "Choose one Health Related Fitness (HRF) component and one Performance Related Fitness (PRF) component from the following:\nbalance coordination flexibility Outline one activity that you could use as a combined approach to training, to improve performance in the two chosen components of fitness. You may use a diagram if you wish.",
        "marks": 8,
        "model": "HRF component: flexibility. PRF component: balance.\nCombined activity: a dynamic single-leg 'clock reach' routine. The athlete stands on one leg with a slight bend at the knee. Imagining a clock face on the floor around them, they reach the free foot out slowly to 12 o'clock, then 3, then 6, then 9, touching the floor lightly at each point and returning to the balanced start position without putting the foot down or losing control. The supporting leg's hip and ankle must move through a large range to allow the reach, and the trunk and hip must stabilise continuously to keep the body upright. Three circuits are performed on each leg, holding the reach position for two seconds at each point.\nIt develops flexibility because the reach takes the hip, hamstrings and calf of both legs through a progressively larger range of movement under control, which is an active rather than a passive stretch. It develops balance simultaneously because the base of support is reduced to one foot and the centre of mass is moved deliberately towards the edge of that base, so the proprioceptors and stabilising muscles of the ankle, knee and hip must work continuously to correct. The two are trained together rather than separately because the flexibility is gained while the body is required to stabilise, which is how both are used in performance. The activity can be progressed by increasing the reach distance, closing the eyes, or standing on an unstable surface such as a foam pad.",
