        "marks": 8,
        "model": "1. Provision and pathways available to boys. Schools and communities generally offer boys a wide range of teams, more fixtures, more coaching and better facility access, and boys' results are given more attention. In most Irish parishes there is a GAA or soccer club with underage teams from primary school upwards, a school team feeding into it, and a visible route from juvenile to adult level. This produces high participation in a small number of team sports, but it also narrows the offering: a boy who does not enjoy or is not selected for those sports may find there is little else provided, which is a significant cause of drop-out for boys who are not among the more able.\n2. Social expectation and hegemonic masculinity. Sport is treated as a normal and expected part of being a boy, so participation carries social approval and boys' participation does not fall in adolescence the way girls' does. But the same expectation restricts what boys take part in and how they behave: activities coded as feminine, such as dance, gymnastics or aerobics, are avoided for fear of ridicule; boys are pushed towards contact and strength sports whether or not these suit them; and the expectation of toughness leads boys to play through injury, conceal pain and anxiety, and train for appearance and size, which is associated with supplement and steroid misuse. Media imagery of a lean, heavily muscular male body reinforces this, motivating some boys to train while causing others who do not resemble it to avoid situations where their body will be seen.",
