        "label": "",
        "question": "List two principles of ethical practice and outline the importance of upholding these principles, in a sporting context.",
        "marks": 8,
        "model": "Principles: integrity and respect.\nIntegrity means acting honestly and consistently with the rules and moral principles of the sport whether or not anyone is watching. Upholding it matters because sport cannot be fully policed: much of an athlete's preparation is unsupervised, officials cannot see every incident, and in self-officiated sports such as golf there is no referee at all. The whole structure — results, records, handicaps, qualification and anti-doping — rests on participants complying when nobody is checking. Where integrity fails, as in the major doping cases in cycling and athletics, every result in the sport becomes questionable, including honest ones, and rebuilding confidence takes a decade or more.\nRespect means treating opponents, teammates, officials and spectators with consideration regardless of the result or of decisions that go against you. Upholding it matters because participation depends on the experience being tolerable. Abuse and dissent are the most cited reason officials leave the game, and a shortage of referees directly reduces the number of fixtures that can be played. Parents will not place children in environments where standards of behaviour are not upheld, and young players copy what they see elite performers do. Respect is also what allows opponents to compete hard and then shake hands, which is the thing that distinguishes sport from conflict.",
