        "label": "(c) (ii)",
        "question": "Describe, using two examples, how body image may influence physical activity participation.",
        "marks": 10,
        "model": "Example 1 — negative body image as a barrier to participation. A teenage girl who believes her body does not match the slim, toned ideal she sees online avoids the situations in sport where her body will be seen: she stops attending PE, avoids swimming because of the swimwear, will not use a gym, and drops out of a club where the kit is tight or short. The barrier is not her physical capacity but her expectation of being judged, and it operates most strongly in exactly the years when participation matters most. The same mechanism affects boys who do not resemble the lean, heavily muscular male ideal and who therefore avoid the gym and the changing room. Governing bodies have responded by relaxing kit rules — permitting leggings and long-sleeved tops and approving the sports hijab — which produced measurable increases in participation, confirming that the barrier was the requirement rather than the activity.\nExample 2 — body image as a distorting motive within participation. A young man who trains primarily to achieve a particular physique organises his training around visible muscle rather than performance or health: he trains only the muscle groups that show, follows a restrictive eating pattern, uses supplements heavily and, at the extreme, considers anabolic steroids. Participation continues but is driven by appearance, which is associated with muscle dysmorphia, disordered eating and overtraining, and it stops abruptly when the desired appearance does not arrive. The same applies in sports where weight is monitored, such as gymnastics, dance and distance running, where appearance-focused pressure is linked to relative energy deficiency in sport, loss of bone density and hormonal disruption. Positive body image works in the opposite direction: a performer who values what their body can do rather than how it looks trains more consistently, is more resilient after setbacks, and is far more likely to still be active in ten years.",
