        "question": "Explain how one type of practice, from the list below, could be used to improve a skill of your choice.\nMassed Practice Part Practice Variable Practice",
        "marks": 8,
        "model": "Type of practice: massed practice. Skill: the basketball free throw.\nMassed practice means repeating the skill continuously in the same unchanging conditions with little or no rest between attempts. The player stands at the free-throw line and takes shot after shot from the same spot, at the same basket, with no defender and no time pressure, following the same pre-shot routine each time.\nIt suits the free throw because the free throw is a closed skill that never varies: the distance, the basket height and the conditions are identical every time it occurs in a game, so there is nothing to be gained by varying the practice. Because the environment never changes, the player can give full attention to grooving the movement pattern — feet set, elbow under the ball, consistent release and follow-through — and the coach can give one specific correction and see it applied on the very next attempt. The high number of repetitions in a short time is what builds the motor programme, and repeating the pre-shot routine alongside the shot means the routine itself becomes automatic, which is what protects the shot under pressure in a game.\nThe limitation is fatigue: once technique begins to deteriorate the player is grooving faults rather than the skill, so the coach must stop the block before quality falls. It is also unsuitable for open skills such as dribbling past a defender, which require variable practice.",
