        "question": "Describe two benefits of physical activity for teenagers.",
        "marks": 8,
        "model": "1. Improved mental health and wellbeing. Adolescence is a period of considerable academic and social pressure, and regular physical activity is associated with lower rates of anxiety and depression, better mood, better sleep and higher self-esteem in teenagers. It also improves concentration and behaviour in school. Being part of a team or club provides belonging and friendship at the stage when peer relationships matter most, and gives a teenager a structure and a trusted adult outside the home, which is associated with lower rates of risky behaviour.\n2. Physical health and long-term habit. Activity in the teenage years builds peak bone mass, which is largely determined by the mid-twenties and protects against osteoporosis for the rest of life, and develops cardiovascular fitness, strength and motor competence at the point where the body adapts most readily. It helps maintain a healthy body composition and lowers the risk of obesity and type 2 diabetes in later life. Just as importantly, the habits and skills formed in adolescence strongly predict adult activity levels, so a teenager who stays active is far more likely to be active at thirty.",
