        "model": "Both performances are judged against artistic and aesthetic criteria — qualities of the movement assessed for how they look and are expressed rather than for an objective outcome such as a time or a score — and both are scored by deducting from a maximum for departures from the ideal, with the difficulty of what is attempted also contributing.\nWhat they share: both are assessed on body line and extension, meaning the shape held in each position with the limbs fully extended and the finish controlled; on control and poise, meaning the movement is executed without wobble, hesitation or over-correction; and on the precision with which the technical requirements of each element are met.\nWhere they differ: the first performance is a composed sequence sustained over time and set to music, so composition and interpretation are themselves judged — the use of the whole performance area, variety of levels and directions, the transitions between elements, musicality, and the projection of character to an audience. The performer must also sustain quality across the full routine while fatiguing, so consistency over duration forms part of the assessment. The second performance is a short, discrete action lasting a second or two, so there is no choreography, no music and no interpretation; the criteria are narrower and almost entirely technical — the approach and take-off, the height achieved, the tightness and clarity of the shape in the air, and the precision of the landing or entry, where judges are effectively assessing a single moment.\nThe practical consequence is that the first rewards a performer's ability to express and connect movement, while the second rewards precision and repeatability, so a performer strong in one would not necessarily score well in the other.",
