        "model": "Strategy: plan recovery into the training schedule and monitor the athlete's response. The coach alternates harder and easier days within the week rather than training hard every session, includes at least one complete rest day, and follows every three or four weeks of progressive loading with a lighter recovery week so the work can be absorbed. Increases in load are made in small planned steps in one variable at a time rather than raising volume and intensity together. Alongside this, the coach records simple markers — resting heart rate, sleep quality, perceived exertion for each session and mood — and reduces the load when they begin to move in the wrong direction. This works because adaptation happens during recovery rather than during the session, so scheduling recovery is what converts training into improvement rather than accumulated fatigue, and monitoring catches the problem while it is still reversible with a few easy days rather than weeks of rest.",
