        "question": "Describe how media coverage is used positively in mass‐participation sports events.\nUse two examples.",
        "marks": 12,
        "model": "Example 1: coverage that shows ordinary participants rather than only the winners. Broadcast and press coverage of an event such as the Dublin City Marathon or the VHI Women's Mini Marathon typically features first-timers, charity runners, older participants and people taking part with a disability, alongside the elite finish. This presents the event as achievable rather than elite, which is precisely what recruits new entrants — organisers report entry surges immediately after coverage, and because these events are annual, coverage of one year drives entries for the next. Social media extends this, because participants share their own involvement and photographs directly, and a friend completing the event is far more persuasive than any advertisement.\nExample 2: coverage that makes the event commercially and practically viable. Media exposure is what a title sponsor is buying, and that sponsorship funds the organisation, the road closures, the safety and medical provision and the charitable proceeds — without it many of these events could not run at their current scale or would have to charge far higher entry fees. Coverage also brings visitors and their spending into the host town, and it gives participants a public occasion to train towards, which is a genuine motivator over the weeks beforehand. Coverage of parkrun in local media works the same way at a smaller scale, publicising the location and time and signalling that beginners are welcome.",
