        "question": "What opportunities can mass participation sporting events provide to businesses that become involved with them?",
        "marks": 5,
        "model": "Mass participation events create a large, identifiable and captive market on a fixed date, and businesses can trade with it in several ways. Local hospitality and retail — hotels, restaurants, cafés and shops — take substantial extra income from thousands of participants and their supporters travelling to and staying in the host town. Sports retailers and service providers sell into a defined need in the weeks beforehand: footwear, clothing, nutrition, training plans, physiotherapy and coaching. The event itself is a business, generating income from entry fees, merchandise, timing services, photography and catering concessions. It is also a valuable sponsorship platform: a brand that titles the event gets its name into every entry, every race number and all the media coverage, reaching an audience it has chosen precisely — health-conscious adults — for a fraction of the cost of general advertising. Because most of these events are annual, businesses can plan around them and build the relationship year on year.",
