      {
        "label": "(d) (i)",
        "question": "The newspaper headline, in Figure 11, refers to Kellie’s confidence, belief and courage. She is also a highly skilled performer with the ability to perform those skills at the highest level.\nExplain two characteristics of a skilled performance.",
        "marks": 5,
        "model": "1. Consistency — a skilled performer reproduces the movement accurately time after time, including under fatigue and pressure. Kellie Harrington's jab lands the same way in the last round of an Olympic final as it does in the gym, which is what makes it reliable enough to build a game plan on.\n2. Anticipation — a skilled performer reads cues early, such as an opponent's shoulder drop or weight shift, and begins their response before the opponent's action is complete. This effectively buys time, which is why a skilled boxer appears to have longer to react than a novice does.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 26,
        "model_source": "scheme"
      },
      {
        "label": "(d) (ii)",
        "question": "skater.\nDiscuss how learning skills effectively can benefit performance.",
        "marks": 5,
        "model": "Learning skills effectively means they become autonomous — performed accurately without conscious attention. The benefits to performance follow from that. First, the movement itself becomes efficient and consistent, so it costs less energy and holds up under fatigue late in a competition. Second, because the skill no longer requires conscious control, the performer's attention is freed for tactical decisions: a skater who no longer thinks about the take-off can concentrate on the routine as a whole and on the judges' criteria, and a games player who does not think about the pass can read the opposition. Third, skills that are well learned are far more resistant to pressure — a performer is much less likely to choke on a movement that is deeply grooved. Fourth, success raises confidence and enjoyment, which sustains motivation to keep training. Finally, sound technique reduces injury risk, because efficient movement loads the body the way it is designed to be loaded.",
        "diagram": "exam-images/pe/2022-OL-fig12.png",
        "source_page": 26,
        "model_source": "scheme"
      }
