        "marks": 4,
        "model": "1. Cost. Club membership, kit, equipment, facility hire and transport all carry a price, and in lower-income households these compete with essential spending. The effect is unequal: children from less well-off families drop out earlier, and sports with high equipment or facility costs — swimming, golf, hockey, rugby — become socially narrow. It also compounds, because a family unable to fund one child's participation often cannot fund any.\n2. Access and facilities. Whether a person takes part depends heavily on what is available within reach: the distance to a pool, hall or pitch, whether there is a club nearby offering their activity, whether public transport or a safe walking and cycling route exists, and whether sessions are scheduled at times people can actually attend. Poor lighting and perceived safety prevent evening activity, and where facilities are booked out by established clubs, casual participants have nowhere to go. Rural areas and disadvantaged urban areas are affected most, and the barrier is structural rather than a matter of individual motivation.",
