        "marks": 8,
        "model": "1. Population-level campaigns and mass participation events. National campaigns and initiatives such as parkrun, Operation Transformation or a national physical activity campaign use media, social media and community organisation to reach very large numbers at low cost per person. Their strength is reach and normalisation: they present activity as ordinary and achievable, give people a specific event and date to train towards, and remove the intimidation of a club by offering a free, non-competitive, welcoming entry point. Their weakness is that they tend to attract people who are already interested, and awareness on its own does not change behaviour unless the opportunity to act on it exists locally.\n2. Environmental and policy approaches. Rather than persuading individuals, this approach changes the conditions in which people live so that activity becomes the easy option: building segregated cycle lanes and greenways, lighting parks and walking routes, guaranteeing timetabled PE and opening school facilities to the community, subsidising club membership, and designing neighbourhoods so that shops and schools are walkable. Its strength is that it reaches everyone, including those who would never respond to a campaign, and the effect is durable because it does not rely on sustaining motivation. Its weakness is that it is expensive, slow, and requires coordination across government departments and local authorities rather than a single agency. The two work best together — a campaign gives people a reason, the environment gives them the opportunity.",
