        "model": "Energy system: the ATP-PC system. Named physical activity: sprinting (100m).\nThe ATP-PC system supplies energy by breaking down phosphocreatine stored in the muscle to resynthesise ATP, without oxygen and without producing fatiguing by-products. It is by far the fastest route to ATP, which is why it is the dominant system in a 100m race. In the blocks and through the acceleration phase the sprinter demands maximal force at maximal rate, and no other system can supply ATP quickly enough to sustain that power output. The system's limitation is capacity rather than rate: stored ATP and phosphocreatine support maximal effort for only around eight to ten seconds, which is why sprinters begin to decelerate slightly in the final twenty metres even as they appear to be running flat out — the phosphocreatine store is depleting and the anaerobic glycolytic system is beginning to contribute. Training implications follow directly: sprint sessions use short maximal efforts with long, full recoveries so that phosphocreatine is fully restored between repetitions, because a repetition performed on an incomplete store is no longer training the system it is meant to train. Creatine supplementation works by increasing the size of that store. The aerobic system matters to the sprinter too, but indirectly — it is what resynthesises phosphocreatine during the recovery between efforts.",
