        "question": "Discuss the difference between vectors and scalars in sport.",
        "marks": 6,
        "model": "A scalar quantity has magnitude only — a size or amount with no direction attached. A vector quantity has both magnitude and direction, so stating the size alone does not describe it fully. In sport the distinction matters because it determines what a measurement actually tells you. Distance and speed are scalars: a runner who covers 400 metres in 49.20 seconds has an average speed of about 8.13 metres per second, and that figure says nothing about which way they went. Displacement and velocity are the corresponding vectors: displacement is the straight-line change in position from start to finish, and velocity is displacement per unit time in a stated direction. On a 400m track this produces a striking result — the athlete's distance is 400 metres but their displacement is close to zero, because they finish near where they started. Force, acceleration, momentum and weight are also vectors, which is why analysis of a sprint start considers not only how much force is applied to the blocks but the direction in which it acts; force applied vertically does not produce forward motion. Mass, time, energy and work are scalars.",
