        "label": "(e)",
        "question": "Mass participation events like the Olympic Games provide great opportunities for business and enterprise.\nAnalyse this statement.",
        "marks": 8,
        "model": "The statement is broadly true, but the benefits are unevenly distributed and come with real costs.\nThe case for it is strong. A mass participation event assembles a very large, identifiable audience at a known place on a known date, which is precisely what businesses need in order to plan. Hospitality and retail in the host city take substantial additional revenue over the period. A supply chain forms around the event itself — construction, security, catering, timing, medical services, transport, merchandise and media production — creating employment before and during it. It is also an unmatched marketing platform: official partners of the Olympic Games pay very large sums for exclusive association with an audience of billions, and smaller events offer the same in miniature to local sponsors targeting a precisely defined market. Broadcast rights and merchandising generate revenue directly. Beyond the event, host cities gain infrastructure and an international profile that can attract tourism and investment for years.\nThe case against is that the returns are frequently overstated. Costs routinely exceed budget, and for major games the public sector carries the risk while private partners take the profit. Much of the spending is displaced rather than new — regular visitors stay away, and local residents avoid the area — and a significant share of the revenue leaves the region with international contractors and sponsors. Purpose-built venues can become expensive liabilities. Small local businesses are often excluded by sponsorship exclusivity rules that protect official partners. Mass participation events at a community scale — a city marathon or a large charity cycle — tend to produce a better and more locally retained return than a mega-event, because they use existing infrastructure and the visitors are largely domestic.\nOn balance the statement holds: these events do create genuine opportunity for business and enterprise, particularly in hospitality, retail, event services and sponsorship. But the scale of the opportunity depends on the event size, on whether existing facilities are used, and on how much of the revenue is retained locally rather than exported.",
