        "question": "Explain three practice methods that can be used to develop skills in a named physical activity of your choice.",
        "marks": 10,
        "model": "Named physical activity: basketball.\n1. Fixed (massed) practice. The skill is repeated in identical, unchanging conditions — free throw after free throw from the same spot, unopposed and with no time pressure. Because the environment never varies, the performer can concentrate entirely on grooving the movement pattern and the coach can correct one cue at a time and see it applied immediately. It suits closed skills whose competitive form never changes, and it is most valuable at the cognitive stage of learning when the motor programme is still being built.\n2. Variable practice. The same skill is repeated in a range of different situations — the lay-up taken from both sides, off either foot, at different speeds and against passive then active defenders. This builds a flexible schema rather than a single fixed pattern, so the performer can adapt the skill to whatever the game presents. It is essential for open skills, where the environment changes constantly, and it produces better transfer into competition than fixed practice even though performance during the session itself looks less consistent.\n3. Distributed practice. The practice is broken into shorter blocks separated by rest or by a different activity, rather than performed as one continuous session. It allows recovery so that repetitions are performed at high quality rather than degrading with fatigue, gives time for feedback to be absorbed and for mental rehearsal, and reduces the risk of injury and of boredom. It is particularly appropriate for skills that are physically or neurally demanding, for beginners whose attention and fitness are limited, and for young players.",
