        "question": "Describe two characteristics of people who positively impact and support the physical activity participation of others.",
        "marks": 8,
        "model": "1. They are inclusive and encouraging rather than selective. Such a person makes everyone feel that the activity is for them — they adapt the session so that participants of different abilities can all take part meaningfully, use positive and specific encouragement, and value effort and improvement rather than only winning. A coach or teacher with this characteristic keeps beginners and late developers involved instead of losing them, which is where most drop-out occurs.\n2. They are approachable and act as a credible role model. They are visibly enthusiastic about the activity and take part themselves, they are trustworthy and consistent, and participants feel able to speak to them about a difficulty. Their own conduct — respecting officials and opponents, accepting decisions, turning up prepared — sets the standard that participants copy. This matters because young people commit to a person before they commit to an activity, and a coach with these characteristics is often the reason a participant stays in a sport.",
