        "model": "Named business: an Irish sports nutrition company supplying batch-tested supplements to athletes and clubs.\nThe business's involvement in sport operates on three fronts. Its core commercial activity is manufacturing and selling protein, creatine and hydration products to athletes, gyms and clubs, and the credibility of that product depends entirely on independent batch certification, since strict liability means a contaminated product ends an athlete's career. Certification is therefore both an ethical obligation and the company's principal point of commercial difference.\nSecond, it invests in sponsorship and endorsement. It supplies products and payment to elite athletes and to national governing bodies in return for association, visible use of the product and appearances. This is marketing rather than philanthropy: the endorsement of a recognisable athlete is what persuades a consumer that the product works, and the return is measured in sales. The relationship carries risk in both directions — an athlete who tests positive damages the brand, and a brand whose product is contaminated destroys the athlete.\nThird, it engages with grassroots sport, supplying club teams and sponsoring underage competitions, which builds long-term brand loyalty in a market where consumers are recruited young.\nEvaluating the involvement: the benefits to sport are real — funding for athletes and NGBs who could not otherwise train full time, and a certified product that reduces the risk of inadvertent doping. The concerns are also real. The marketing is directed at young men, most of whom would gain more from a better diet and more sleep than from any supplement; the evidence base supports only a handful of products; and the sponsorship of underage sport normalises supplement use at an age when governing bodies discourage it entirely. There is also an inherent tension in a business whose income depends on athletes believing they need something that, for most, is unnecessary. On balance the involvement is legitimate and adds value where the product is certified and honestly marketed, but it requires the governing bodies accepting the sponsorship to set limits on how and to whom it is promoted.",
